WEBVTT

00:00:00.126 --> 00:00:02.662
Storage type is an engineering decision.

00:00:02.662 --> 00:00:06.997
Identifiers can use text, UUID, or integer keys depending on the system.

00:00:06.997 --> 00:00:12.262
Counts use integer types. Currency often uses exact NUMERIC storage.

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:14.242
Dates and event times should use temporal types.

00:00:14.242 --> 00:00:20.157
The important idea is that the database representation should support the meaning and quality rules of the

00:00:20.157 --> 00:00:25.482
data. A technically valid storage type is not enough if it encourages incorrect interpretation.

00:00:25.482 --> 00:00:30.077
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:30.077 --> 00:00:35.772
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:35.772 --> 00:00:39.107
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:39.107 --> 00:00:44.447
In a real setting such as a hospital combining patient tables, device JSON, and clinical notes,

00:00:44.447 --> 00:00:50.922
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:50.922 --> 00:00:53.232
before discussing syntax.

00:00:53.232 --> 00:00:58.720
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
